# Custom Fedora Silverblue image for the ThinkPad — "bluefin-style".
#
# A thin layer over stock Silverblue. The active host-level changes:
#   - drop `toolbox` (ships IN the base image) and add `distrobox`.
#   - install the compositors (niri + Hyprland) from COPR. (desktoppak, the
#     old per-user bwrap payload layer, was retired 2026-09-01 — the normal
#     bootc/image path won; see host-image/README.md.)
#
# This image uses the modern bootc-native build model, matching current
# Bluefin/finpilot (projectbluefin/finpilot):
#   - `dnf5` for all package changes at build time (NOT `rpm-ostree`).
#     finpilot's AGENTS.md is explicit: "Use dnf5 exclusively (never dnf, yum,
#     rpm-ostree)". In a bootc container build dnf5 writes straight into the
#     image's /usr, so a plain `dnf5 remove` drops a base package — no
#     `rpm-ostree override remove` needed (that was an rpm-ostree *client*
#     concept; at build time the tree is just a writable filesystem).
#   - `bootc container lint` to finalize/validate (replaces the old
#     `ostree container commit`).
#   - Switched/deployed with `bootc`, not `rpm-ostree` (see switch.sh).
#   bootc still uses ostree/libostree under the hood — it's the stable,
#   future-facing front-end (per bootc-status(8): "bootc and rpm-ostree share
#   the same code").
#
# Built ON BEES (daily timer + on-demand `cjust image-rebuild`) and published
# to the homelab zot registry; the thinkpad pulls from there. Local
# build.sh/switch.sh remain as the break-glass path. See README.md.
#
# Base image: the same one ublue/bluefin/finpilot derive from.

ARG FEDORA_MAJOR_VERSION=44
FROM quay.io/fedora-ostree-desktops/silverblue:${FEDORA_MAJOR_VERSION}

# ---- 1. The swap: toolbox -> distrobox --------------------------------
# `dnf5 remove` drops a base-image package; `dnf5 install` pulls distrobox from
# the Fedora repos. The cache mounts persist the dnf cache across rebuilds so
# repeated builds are fast (finpilot/lina-bh pattern).
#
# NOTE: this does NOT change the bespoke dev container in ../toolbox (that one
# stays a plain podman create with a separate $HOME). distrobox here just gives
# the `distrobox` CLI on the host for ad-hoc containers, replacing `toolbox`.
#
# `just` and `fzf` are host-native on purpose: they back the `cjust` task menu
# (hosts/thinkpad/Justfile + the cjust function in bubblebox/files/.zshrc). Like
# oh-my-posh they can't live in a bubblebox — `cjust` must run before any box
# is set up. (`just` was previously borrowed from the `default` box, which
# made the menu depend on the thing it bootstraps; `fzf` backs `just --choose`.)
# `nodejs` + `npm` are host-native for the hermes desktop app build
# (`cjust hermes-desktop-build` runs npm directly; see Justfile).
RUN --mount=type=cache,dst=/var/cache/libdnf5 \
    --mount=type=cache,dst=/var/cache/rpm-ostree \
    dnf5 -y install distrobox zsh just fzf nodejs npm

# ---- 2. Desktop stack: niri + our niri-caelestia-shell fork --------------
# niri comes from the upstream maintainer's COPR (yalter/niri), which tracks
# newer than Fedora's package. xwayland-satellite provides niri's X11 app
# support (niri has no native XWayland). dnf5-plugins supplies the `dnf5 copr`
# subcommand used below; it is installed in the same transaction, so the
# subcommand is available to the very next `&&` in this RUN. The cache mounts
# keep COPR downloads fast across rebuilds, matching step 1.
#
# Hyprland + Caelestia-shell were REMOVED 2026-09-02 (trial concluded; the
# keeper is niri + our own fork of niri-caelestia-shell, see below). satty,
# dart-sass, and the mineiro/hyprland COPR were Hyprland-trial baggage and
# leave with it. peterwu/rendezvous stays: it only ever supplied
# bibata-cursor-themes, which the cursor setup (niri xcursor-theme + user
# gsettings) still uses.
#
# The shell stack for niri (fork: github.com/chad-russell/niri-caelestia-shell,
# our maintenance fork of AyushKr2003's archived port of Caelestia to niri):
#   - `quickshell` from errornointernet/quickshell (F44 release build 0.3.x —
#     the QML runtime the fork's shell runs on).
#   - fonts + materialyoucolor from gmanka/caelestia — the ONE thing we keep
#     that COPR for (material-symbols-fonts, cascadia-code-nerd-fonts,
#     python3-materialyoucolor); caelestia-shell itself is NOT installed.
#   - `app2unit` from celestelove/app2unit (the fork's app-launcher helper:
#     launches apps as transient user units, scoped to the graphical session).
#   - `libcava` (+ devel, tiny) from celestelove/libcava — the audio-
#     visualizer lib the shell's C++ plugin links for the audio visualizer
#     widget (plugin built in step 2.5 below).
#
# Runtime deps for the fork (each verified available on F44):
#   matugen (Fedora proper — the Material-You color pipeline), nmcli via
#   NetworkManager (base image), kdialog + kf6-kirigami + kf6-syntax-
#   highlighting + plasma-integration (Qt dialogs/theme), swappy +
#   tesseract-langpack-eng + wf-recorder (screenshot annotate/OCR/record),
#   ydotool/wtype (virtual input), translate-shell, cava (CLI visualizer),
#   libqalculate + qalculate (launcher calc mode), pavucontrol, lm_sensors,
#   geoclue2 (weather location), fish (launcher calc mode), jq/bc/rsync/
#   xdg-user-dirs/xdg-utils (shell scripts), nm-connection-editor (tray NM
#   state). Portals: the niri-friendly set (gnome screen-share + gtk).
#
# NO dolphin/system-settings: their weak-dep chain pulls in the entire Plasma
# workspace (plasma.desktop session included). nautilus (GNOME base) is the
# file manager; the fork's file-manager config default just points at a
# binary — nautilus covers it. install_weak_deps=False keeps other recommends
# (fonts, extra modules) from ballooning the image the same way.
#
# COSMIC soft-removed 2026-09-01: not in active use; revisit in a few months
# to check epoch progress. To restore, un-comment the cosmic lines below and
# rebuild (`cjust image-rebuild`). Settings are already backed up in-repo
# (cjust cosmic-backup → bubblebox/files/.config/cosmic/).
RUN --mount=type=cache,dst=/var/cache/libdnf5 \
    --mount=type=cache,dst=/var/cache/rpm-ostree \
    dnf5 -y install dnf5-plugins \
    && dnf5 -y copr enable yalter/niri \
    # && dnf5 -y copr enable ryanabx/cosmic-epoch \
    && dnf5 -y copr enable errornointernet/quickshell \
    && dnf5 -y copr enable gmanka/caelestia \
    && dnf5 -y copr enable celestelove/libcava \
    && dnf5 -y copr enable celestelove/app2unit \
    && dnf5 -y copr enable peterwu/rendezvous \
    && dnf5 -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False install \
                       niri xwayland-satellite bibata-cursor-themes \
                       quickshell app2unit libcava libcava-devel \
                       material-symbols-fonts cascadia-code-nerd-fonts \
                       python3-materialyoucolor matugen \
                       kf6-kirigami kf6-syntax-highlighting kdialog plasma-integration \
                       qt6-qt5compat qt6-qtmultimedia qt6-qtquicktimeline \
                       wl-clipboard cliphist grim slurp swappy \
                       tesseract tesseract-langpack-eng wf-recorder \
                       ydotool wtype translate-shell cava \
                       libqalculate qalculate pavucontrol lm_sensors \
                       geoclue2 \
                       xdg-desktop-portal-gnome xdg-desktop-portal-gtk \
                       fish jq bc curl wget ripgrep rsync \
                       xdg-user-dirs xdg-utils libnotify brightnessctl \
                       ddcutil playerctl upower nm-connection-editor \
                       breeze-icon-theme
    # && dnf5 -y install cosmic-desktop

# ---- 2.5. Build + install our niri-caelestia-shell fork ------------------
# The shell is a Quickshell config (pure QML) + a small C++ Quickshell plugin
# (libcaelestia: qalculate wrapper, image analysis, niri IPC, cava/audio
# providers) + `caelestia` version helper. We build it from OUR fork
# (chad-russell/niri-caelestia-shell — maintenance fork of AyushKr2003's
# archived niri port of Caelestia) at a pinned commit, same model as a
# distro package: system-wide install (plugin → /usr/lib/qt6/qml/Caelestia,
# helper → /usr/lib/caelestia, shell QML → /etc/xdg/quickshell/
# niri-caelestia-shell), no user-clone needed to run it. Iterating on the
# shell happens in a user clone at ~/.config/quickshell/niri-caelestia-shell
# (same config name shadows the system copy — that's the Quickshell lookup
# order); the image copy is the stable baseline.
#
# Pin is deliberate: bump on purpose, not by drift. Rebuilds are cached
# by the dnf layer below this one; this layer recompiles only when the pin
# moves (~2-3 min). The toolchain + *-devel packages stay in the image on
# purpose: this is the machine we iterate on the shell from, so building
# the C++ plugin on-host is a feature, not bloat.
#
# NOTE on prefixes: upstream's CMake install dirs are RELATIVE ("usr/lib/...,
# etc/xdg/..."), so the prefix MUST be "/" (their README's invocation) —
# CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr yields /usr/usr/... and /usr/etc/..., the latter
# failing `bootc container lint` (etc-usretc). Two more overrides, both
# required on Fedora (upstream defaults are Arch-flavored):
#   - INSTALL_QMLDIR must be /usr/lib64/qt6/qml (Fedora's Qt6 QML2_IMPORT_DIR;
#     the default usr/lib/qt6/qml is Arch's layout — landed there on first
#     build attempt and `import Caelestia` would fail at runtime).
#   - INSTALL_QSCONFDIR is the fork's canonical config name so `qs -c
#     niri-caelestia-shell` (spawn line in niri config.kdl + ipc binds)
#     resolves against this install.
ADD https://github.com/chad-russell/niri-caelestia-shell/archive/bf2620b936dc9e6741d7874475723776dc6f3ada.tar.gz /tmp/ncs.tar.gz
RUN tar -xzf /tmp/ncs.tar.gz -C /tmp \
    && dnf5 -y install cmake ninja-build gcc-c++ \
                       qt6-qtbase-devel qt6-qtdeclarative-devel \
                       qt6-qt5compat-devel qt6-qtmultimedia-devel \
                       fftw-devel libqalculate-devel pipewire-devel \
                       aubio-devel \
    && cmake -S /tmp/niri-caelestia-shell-bf2620b936dc9e6741d7874475723776dc6f3ada \
           -B /tmp/ncs-build \
           -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/ \
           -DINSTALL_QMLDIR=usr/lib64/qt6/qml \
           -DINSTALL_QSCONFDIR=etc/xdg/quickshell/niri-caelestia-shell \
           -DDISTRIBUTOR="cn image build" \
    && cmake --build /tmp/ncs-build \
    && cmake --install /tmp/ncs-build \
    && rm -rf /tmp/ncs.tar.gz /tmp/niri-caelestia-shell-bf2620b936dc9e6741d7874475723776dc6f3ada /tmp/ncs-build

# ---- 3. SELinux: disabled (personal-laptop host policy) -----------------
# This is a single-user personal laptop, and the operator comes from NixOS/
# Arch where SELinux isn't part of the workflow. Fedora ships the `targeted`
# policy enabled by default; its friction (label mismatches on relocated paths,
# audit noise, per-inode relabeling cost) isn't worth the hardening for this
# threat model — the high-value apps (Noctalia/Vicinae) are already sandboxed
# by the bwrap wrappers above, and the daemons `targeted` most protects
# (httpd, named, ...) don't run on a desktop. So: disable it in the image.
#
# Mechanism: `SELINUX=disabled` in /etc/selinux/config — the canonical, image-
# bakeable way. The selinux-policy packages stay installed (removing them
# breaks a web of dependencies), but no policy is loaded and no enforcement
# happens, so the host-session features layered on top (the Noctalia/Vicinae
# bwrap wrappers, which relocate home paths behind tmpfs + bind mounts) need no
# SELinux-aware machinery at all. For absolute-
# zero kernel overhead you could additionally
# `rpm-ostree kargs --append-if-missing selinux=0` on the host, but the config
# change alone is effectively all the way there.
# Make zsh the default login shell for newly-created users, while keeping bash
# available (it stays installed as part of the base image). The default user on
# this host is created by the installer from these defaults, so new installs get
# zsh automatically. For an already-installed host, switch the existing account
# with `chsh -s /usr/bin/zsh` after deploying this image.
#
# shadow-utils ships the file at /etc/default/useradd, but in an ostree/bootc
# image the "default" copy may also live at /usr/etc/default/useradd (the image
# default that gets copied to /etc on first boot). Handle either location.
RUN set -e; \
    edited=0; \
    for f in /etc/default/useradd /usr/etc/default/useradd; do \
        if [ -f "$f" ]; then \
            sed -i 's|^SHELL=.*|SHELL=/usr/bin/zsh|' "$f"; \
            grep -q '^SHELL=/usr/bin/zsh' "$f"; \
            edited=1; \
        fi; \
    done; \
    [ "$edited" = 1 ]

RUN sed -i 's/^SELINUX=.*/SELINUX=disabled/' /etc/selinux/config && \
    grep -q '^SELINUX=disabled' /etc/selinux/config

# ---- 3.5. kernel args (bootc static kargs) -----------------------------
# bootc applies *.toml files under /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d/ to every deployment
# staged from this image — on BOTH `bootc install` and routine `bootc upgrade`
# — by diffing the booted image's kargs.d against the new image's and patching
# the new deployment's cmdline accordingly. Removing a file from a future
# image retracts its karg on the next upgrade. This is the image-bakeable,
# declarative equivalent of `rpm-ostree kargs --append-if-missing`, and it
# survives reinstalls. (Schema: a required `kargs` array of strings plus an
# optional `match-architectures` array; confirmed against bootc_kargs.rs in
# bootc 1.15.2.)
#
# usbcore.autosuspend=-1: disables USB runtime autosuspend globally. The Dell
# P3225QE over USB-C/DisplayPort on this Lunar Lake (xe) ThinkPad was flapping
# the DP-1 connector at a steady ~3s cadence — the classic signature of the
# USB-C DP tunnel cycling in/out of runtime suspend, which drops HPD and forces
# a link retrain each wake. Keeping the tunnel awake fixes the external-monitor
# black-screen cycling at full 4K@60, so the 1440p workaround in
# bubblebox/files/.config/monitors.xml + niri config can be reverted once this is live.
RUN mkdir -p /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d && \
    printf 'kargs = ["usbcore.autosuspend=-1"]\nmatch-architectures = ["x86_64"]\n' \
    > /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d/10-usb-autosuspend.toml

# ---- 3.6. insecure-registry drop-in for the homelab zot registry --------
# The host's boot image pulls from bees's zot registry at 10.10.0.6:5000 —
# plain HTTP inside the Nebula WireGuard tunnel (Nebula IS the transport
# security; the registry is bound to the overlay address only). This drop-in
# teaches container tools in the image (skopeo/bootc container previews,
# `podman pull 10.10.0.6:5000/...` if you ever want to inspect the image
# without switching) that the registry is intentionally insecure-HTTP.
# NOTE: bootc itself reads these drop-ins too (same containers-image stack)
# — trust for 10.10.0.6:5000 must exist on the HOST at switch/upgrade time;
# switch.sh bootstraps it for the running host.
RUN mkdir -p /etc/containers/registries.conf.d && \
    printf '[[registry]]\nlocation = "10.10.0.6:5000"\ninsecure = true\n' \
    > /etc/containers/registries.conf.d/10-homelab-registry.conf

# ---- 3.7. Nebula overlay hostnames (/etc/hosts) --------------------
# The fleet's NixOS hosts get these /etc/hosts entries via
# modules/nebula-hosts.nix, rendered from lib/host-meta.nix (the single
# source of truth). The thinkpad bakes the SAME rendered entries into the
# image's /etc/hosts. At deploy time bootc migrates the image's /etc into
# the deployment's /usr/etc defaults, and ostree 3-way merges those into
# the live /etc/hosts on every upgrade — so overlay names (bee, bees, nas,
# nebula-hetzner, ...) resolve everywhere, and future host-meta changes
# flow to this host with each image upgrade, as long as the live /etc/hosts
# stays unmodified (it ships unmodified today).
#
# MECHANISM — COPY, not RUN: buildah RUN-time-masks /etc/hosts (like
# resolv.conf / hostname): every RUN container gets a freshly generated
# /etc/hosts, so `RUN cat >> /etc/hosts` writes the throwaway runtime copy
# and NEVER commits to the layer (verified live: a separate-RUN grep
# assertion failed, 2026-08-27). COPY writes the layer tarball directly —
# no masking. etc-hosts is the COMPLETE file (stock Fedora loopback block +
# the generated fragment), rendered by `nix run .#render-thinkpad-hosts`;
# the identical fragment (hosts-sync on the laptop) is also committed as
# nebula-hosts. `nix flake check` fails if either goes stale.
COPY etc-hosts /etc/hosts

# ---- 4. build identity (lets `bootc upgrade` detect a rebuild) ----------
# Each build injects a unique IMAGE_VERSION (git SHA + timestamp; see
# build.sh) two ways:
#   - as an OCI LABEL, which lives in the image *config* and therefore changes
#     the image's MANIFEST/CONFIG DIGEST on every build. That digest is exactly
#     what `bootc upgrade` compares against the booted image, so a rebuilt
#     `:44` is always detected as new and gets deployed (see README, "switch
#     once, upgrade thereafter"). Without the label, a rebuild that reuses
#     podman's layer cache has an identical manifest digest and `bootc upgrade`
#     correctly — but uselessly — reports "No update available".
#   - written to /usr as well, so on the booted system you can `cat
#     /usr/lib/host-image-thinkpad-version` to see exactly which build you're
#     on without querying bootc/podman.
# This is the ONLY layer that changes per build, so the dnf layers above stay
# cached and rebuilds stay fast.
ARG IMAGE_VERSION=local
RUN printf 'IMAGE_VERSION=%s\n' "${IMAGE_VERSION}" > /usr/lib/host-image-thinkpad-version
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.version="${IMAGE_VERSION}"


# ---- 5. finalize: bootc-native validation -----------------------------
# systemd as pid 1 is required for a bootc image. `bootc container lint` then
# validates the image (catches /run leftovers, /opt issues, etc.) before you
# ever switch to it — it replaces the old `ostree container commit`.
CMD ["/sbin/init"]
RUN bootc container lint
